WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:04.525
Seven calls to nasm, one call to ld, and no compiler anywhere.

00:00:04.905 --> 00:00:09.239
What comes out is a static binary with no dynamic section.

00:00:09.239 --> 00:00:11.997
Then it answers a question, as Qwen3-0.6B.

00:00:14.835 --> 00:00:15.359
I'm Mnemonic.

00:00:15.359 --> 00:00:17.981
Let's open the binary and see what does the work.

00:00:23.783 --> 00:00:28.027
Before any thread wakes, the activation vector becomes int8, with one scale

00:00:28.027 --> 00:00:29.441
per block of thirty-two.

00:00:29.861 --> 00:00:32.164
The multiply instruction wants one unsigned side.

00:00:32.164 --> 00:00:36.769
So take the absolute value of x, and push its sign onto the weights.

00:00:37.189 --> 00:00:40.417
Thirty-two byte products, folded pairwise into sixteen lanes.

00:00:40.417 --> 00:00:44.049
Both sides are clamped, so the sums never overflow.

00:00:44.469 --> 00:00:48.031
Multiply by ones to fold again, into eight exact integers.

00:00:48.469 --> 00:00:52.484
Convert to float, multiply by the two block scales, and add into

00:00:52.484 --> 00:00:53.823
one of four accumulators.

00:00:55.358 --> 00:00:59.778
llama.cpp uses the same sign trick and the same two instructions, one

00:00:59.778 --> 00:01:02.725
block at a time into a single accumulator.

00:01:03.145 --> 00:01:07.340
llm.asm unrolls four blocks into four accumulators, over rows that start

00:01:07.340 --> 00:01:08.866
on a cache line.

00:01:10.258 --> 00:01:13.399
I ran it myself on a busy desktop chip.

00:01:13.399 --> 00:01:17.587
At four threads the integer kernel decoded about one and a half

00:01:17.587 --> 00:01:18.983
times faster than llama.cpp.

00:01:20.558 --> 00:01:22.899
A thread is a raw clone call with its own stack.

00:01:23.299 --> 00:01:26.395
Workers spin on a counter, so a new job costs no syscall.

00:01:26.795 --> 00:01:29.583
Only when the user goes quiet do they sleep on a futex.

00:01:29.983 --> 00:01:33.362
Each one reports done on its own cache line, so no

00:01:33.362 --> 00:01:34.590
counter bounces between cores.

00:01:34.990 --> 00:01:38.377
Every row belongs to one thread, so the output is identical

00:01:38.377 --> 00:01:39.608
at any thread count.

00:01:40.008 --> 00:01:41.026
But nobody yields.

00:01:41.026 --> 00:01:45.096
On my shared machine, with more threads than free cores, every barrier

00:01:45.096 --> 00:01:47.810
waited on a descheduled worker, and decode collapsed.

00:01:49.608 --> 00:01:52.604
Both engines climb, then flatten past sixteen threads.

00:01:53.024 --> 00:01:54.565
That plateau is memory.

00:01:54.565 --> 00:01:59.188
At its peak, llm.asm streams about eighty gigabytes a second of weights.

00:02:01.033 --> 00:02:03.134
Then the second post deleted Linux.

00:02:03.134 --> 00:02:06.986
A diskless VM, two gigabytes, every core near a hundred percent.

00:02:07.366 --> 00:02:08.956
It boots straight into a chat.

00:02:08.956 --> 00:02:11.340
With no scheduler, a core spins or it works.

00:02:12.908 --> 00:02:16.660
The kernel maps the first four gigabytes in two-megabyte pages and finds

00:02:16.660 --> 00:02:18.537
the weights at a physical address.

00:02:18.917 --> 00:02:22.528
It wakes every core over the APIC and prints its own timings.

00:02:24.108 --> 00:02:26.466
Most of the headline runs on Linux already.

00:02:26.466 --> 00:02:30.002
The OS adds the last slice, and huge pages on Linux should

00:02:30.002 --> 00:02:31.475
win most of that back.

00:02:31.895 --> 00:02:34.080
Linux build vs llama.cpp.

00:02:34.604 --> 00:02:36.077
OS vs Linux build.

00:02:37.313 --> 00:02:39.030
Together, in a KVM guest.

00:02:41.608 --> 00:02:44.817
Hugging Face splits the text with a regex first, and the last

00:02:44.817 --> 00:02:46.155
space stays with the word.

00:02:46.575 --> 00:02:48.093
The assembly skips that split.

00:02:48.093 --> 00:02:51.735
On my run, five of twelve strings came out different, every one

00:02:51.735 --> 00:02:52.949
a run of whitespace.

00:02:54.683 --> 00:02:58.507
Split rows, fused projections, four accumulators, one owner per row.

00:02:58.507 --> 00:03:00.419
None of it needs assembly.

00:03:02.233 --> 00:03:06.974
llm.asm: twelve kilobytes that read six hundred megabytes, every token.

00:03:07.394 --> 00:03:09.521
Every source is in the full article.

00:03:09.521 --> 00:03:10.129
I'm Mnemonic.

00:03:10.129 --> 00:03:10.433
Bye!
